\description{
Renders a publication-ready Pearson correlation matrix across
two or more classification images (CIs), with optional face
region masking and optional direct save to PNG (600 dpi) or
PDF.

Designed for the common publication-figure task: given a small
set of group-mean CIs from different conditions, show how
similar they are pixel-wise. A stage-2 consumer: accepts a
\code{\link[=group_ci]{group_ci()}} result directly, a named numeric matrix of
\verb{pixels x n_groups}, or a named list of CIs (vectors, single-
column matrices, or per-producer \code{signal_matrix} objects from
\verb{ci_from_responses_*()} that are reduced to group means
internally). Mixed shapes inside the list are allowed.

Diverging-palette convention matches the rest of the package:
positive \code{r} = blue, negative \code{r} = red, neutral color at
zero. The color scale is fixed at \code{c(-1, 1)} so panels are
directly comparable across runs and across paper figures.
}
\section{Reading the plot}{

Each cell shows the Pearson correlation \code{r} between two
group-mean CIs computed across the pixels included in \code{mask}
(all pixels by default). Saturation encodes \verb{|r|}; hue encodes
sign (blue = positive, red = negative). Numbers in each cell
show the exact \code{r} to \code{value_digits} decimal places.

The diagonal is identically 1 by definition. With
\code{triangle = "full"} the diagonal is rendered as a saturated
blue cell with \code{+1.00} inside. With \code{triangle = "upper"} or
\code{triangle = "lower"} the diagonal cells are blank and carry
the CI labels instead, freeing the figure margins.
}

\section{Pearson r between CIs is not a clean similarity score}{

Two base-subtracted CIs share image-domain spatial structure
(face shape, oval signal support) that pushes their
correlation above zero even when the underlying mental
representations are unrelated. A high absolute \code{r} does not
by itself license a similarity claim. Use this correlogram to
visualise the \strong{relative} ordering of pair-wise correlations
(which pairs covary more than others), and benchmark any
absolute claim against a permutation null. For a
baseline-free magnitude summary of how different two
conditions are, see \code{\link[=rel_dissimilarity]{rel_dissimilarity()}} (Euclidean
distance with bootstrap CI). For pixel-level localisation
of \emph{where} two conditions differ, see \code{\link[=rel_cluster_test]{rel_cluster_test()}}.
}

